// coc.nvim settings — https://github.com/neoclide/coc.nvim
// (coc reads this as JSONC, so // comments and trailing commas are allowed.)
//
// WHERE TO PUT THIS FILE
//   coc loads "coc-settings.json" from your nvim config dir:
//       ~/.config/nvim/coc-settings.json
//   Either copy it there, or open/edit it from inside nvim with:
//       :CocConfig
//   This repo copy is a TEMPLATE. The live file is machine-specific
//   (absolute path below), so don't symlink it the way init.vim is linked —
//   copy it and fill in the path for the current machine.
//   After editing, reload it in nvim with:  :CocRestart
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS
//   The coc-flutter-tools extension (a fork of coc-flutter) tries to locate
//   the Dart SDK by running `which dart`. When Flutter's bin dir is on PATH,
//   that resolves to Flutter's `dart` wrapper (e.g. ~/flutter/bin/dart) and
//   the extension then looks for the analysis server at
//       <flutter>/bin/snapshots/analysis_server.dart.snapshot
//   But Flutter's real Dart SDK is nested, so it actually lives at
//       <flutter>/bin/cache/dart-sdk/bin/snapshots/analysis_server.dart.snapshot
//   The check fails, the LSP never starts ("flutter SDK not found"), and
//   autocomplete dies (diagnostics from another LSP may still show up).
//
//   THE FIX: tell the extension the Flutter SDK root explicitly via
//   "flutter.sdk.path". When set to a valid SDK root (a dir containing
//   bin/flutter), the extension derives the analyzer from
//   <root>/bin/cache/dart-sdk and skips the broken `which dart` guess.
//   Set it to the Flutter root on the current machine, e.g. ~/flutter
//   (use an absolute path; this key is not ~-expanded).
{
  "flutter.sdk.path": "/ABSOLUTE/PATH/TO/flutter"
}
